1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,482
Hello.

2
00:00:00,482 --> 00:00:08,677
My name is Rakhman Seidigali, from group S E twenty four eleven at Astana I T University.

3
00:00:08,677 --> 00:00:12,052
This is WeatherVision, my Computer Vision project.

4
00:00:12,052 --> 00:00:17,836
It classifies outdoor photographs into four categories: Cloudy, Rain, Shine, and Sunrise.

5
00:00:17,836 --> 00:00:24,103
The application brings together three trained models, a comparison of their results, and

6
00:00:24,103 --> 00:00:25,067
visual explanations.

7
00:00:25,067 --> 00:00:30,370
This demonstration shows the running application and its saved experimental results.

8
00:00:31,370 --> 00:00:36,484
The project uses the Multi-class Weather Dataset published by Gbeminiyi Ajayi.

9
00:00:36,484 --> 00:00:41,598
After duplicate removal, it contains one thousand and ninety seven photographs.

10
00:00:41,598 --> 00:00:48,107
There are seven hundred and sixty eight training images, one hundred and sixty four

11
00:00:48,107 --> 00:00:52,756
validation images, and one hundred and sixty five test images.

12
00:00:52,756 --> 00:00:55,545
All experiments use the same split.

13
00:00:55,545 --> 00:01:00,659
The Simple C N N learns three convolutional blocks from scratch.

14
00:01:00,659 --> 00:01:03,913
MobileNet version two reuses efficient ImageNet features.

15
00:01:03,913 --> 00:01:08,562
ConvNext Tiny uses deeper convolutional stages and larger spatial filters.

16
00:01:08,562 --> 00:01:14,606
For both pretrained networks, only the new four class classification head is trained.

17
00:01:14,606 --> 00:01:19,720
Training augmentation includes horizontal flips, small rotations, and mild color changes.

18
00:01:20,720 --> 00:01:24,130
I now choose Upload and select a weather photograph.

19
00:01:24,130 --> 00:01:27,539
The preview lets me check the input before prediction.

20
00:01:27,539 --> 00:01:30,570
I select ConvNext Tiny and press Classify image.

21
00:01:30,570 --> 00:01:34,737
The application loads its saved checkpoint and displays the predicted category.

22
00:01:34,737 --> 00:01:40,799
The confidence is the model's softmax score, and the four bars show how the scores are

23
00:01:40,799 --> 00:01:42,314
distributed across the labels.

24
00:01:42,314 --> 00:01:45,345
The inference backend here is C P U.

25
00:01:45,345 --> 00:01:51,028
The timing panel shows a single forward pass and the average of thirty measurements after

26
00:01:51,028 --> 00:01:52,543
five warm up runs.

27
00:01:52,543 --> 00:01:56,331
Model loading and image preparation are excluded from this benchmark.

28
00:01:56,331 --> 00:01:59,741
The environment panel identifies the software and the browser.

29
00:01:59,741 --> 00:02:05,045
Next, Compare on this image runs all three model families on the same input.

30
00:02:05,045 --> 00:02:09,212
The comparison can be downloaded as a C S V file.

31
00:02:09,212 --> 00:02:12,243
For ConvNext, Show Grad Cam adds an explanation.

32
00:02:12,243 --> 00:02:15,274
The original image appears beside the heatmap overlay.

33
00:02:15,274 --> 00:02:19,062
Brighter regions indicate spatial features associated with the predicted class.

34
00:02:19,062 --> 00:02:24,366
This is an approximate attribution, rather than a precise segmentation of clouds or rain.

35
00:02:24,366 --> 00:02:32,322
I can download the class scores as J S O N and save the overlay as a P N G image.

36
00:02:32,322 --> 00:02:36,110
Switching the model keeps valid results for the same photograph.

37
00:02:50,720 --> 00:02:56,473
The Experiments tab compares all four training configurations on the same one hundred and

38
00:02:56,473 --> 00:02:58,117
sixty five test images.

39
00:02:58,117 --> 00:03:03,048
Simple C N N reaches ninety five point one five percent accuracy.

40
00:03:03,048 --> 00:03:06,335
MobileNet reaches ninety six point three six percent.

41
00:03:06,335 --> 00:03:12,088
ConvNext with augmentation reaches ninety eight point one eight percent, with macro F one

42
00:03:12,088 --> 00:03:14,964
of zero point nine eight two seven.

43
00:03:14,964 --> 00:03:19,073
Without augmentation, ConvNext reaches ninety seven point five eight percent.

44
00:03:19,073 --> 00:03:24,415
The difference is one additional correctly classified image: one hundred and sixty two

45
00:03:24,415 --> 00:03:27,292
instead of one hundred and sixty one.

46
00:03:27,292 --> 00:03:30,579
The learning curves show training and validation behavior.

47
00:03:30,579 --> 00:03:35,510
The confusion matrix shows true labels in rows and predictions in columns.

48
00:03:36,510 --> 00:03:40,710
ConvNext with augmentation has the highest accuracy in this comparison.

49
00:03:40,710 --> 00:03:47,430
The small C N N provides the shortest measured forward time on Apple M P S.

50
00:03:47,430 --> 00:03:53,730
The test set is small, and ambiguous labels or unusual lighting can affect predictions in

51
00:03:53,730 --> 00:03:54,570
new environments.

52
00:03:54,570 --> 00:03:58,350
The Project tab contains dataset attribution and both reports.

53
00:03:58,350 --> 00:04:02,970
Source code, configurations, an executed notebook, and downloadable model weights are

54
00:04:02,970 --> 00:04:05,490
available in the public GitHub repository.

55
00:04:05,490 --> 00:04:07,170
Thank you for watching.
